Non-normative notes on why Danish dates work the way they do.  For a possible
transformation primer.

Danish dates

The addition of Danish dates to the registry follows the policy that
transformation input types will be sufficiently constrained to ensure a unique
input value, but no further.  

Thus, the ordinalisation which is standard on Danish dates is treated as part
of the delimiter, and only enforced to the extent that a delimiter is required.
So "13. august" is the expectation, but "13 august" and "13.august" are also
accepted.  "13august", however, is not.

We have allowed months to be abbreviated in unexpected ways, so long as the
meaning of the input value is unique.  So "sep. 2012" is expected, but "sept.
2012" is also accepted.  Only the first three characters of the month are
tested, so nonsense like "AUGXXX 2012" is also allowed.

Chinese, Japanese and Korean dates

A number of date types were defined in version 2 of the Transformation
Registry, for generic Chinese, Japanese and Korean dates, and for Japanese era
dates.  These follow a stricter regime than we have adopted for
Western-language dates, and we feel that this is justified by general usage in
these countries.
